The polyethylene repeat unit is \(\mathrm{C_2H_4}\), and the standard orthorhombic PE unit cell contains 2 repeat units. Using the common rounded polymerization energy of ethylene, \(\approx 100\ \mathrm{kJ/mol}\) per \(\mathrm{C_2H_4}\):

\[
E = 2 \times \frac{100\ \mathrm{kJ/mol}}{96.485\ \mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}\,eV^{-1}}}
\approx 2.07\ \mathrm{eV}
\]

The reaction is exothermic, so the energy change is \(-2.07\) eV; the energy involved in magnitude is:

\[
\boxed{2.07}
\]

For crystalline polyethylene, use the conventional orthorhombic unit cell:

- \(a \approx 7.40\ \text{Å},\ b \approx 4.95\ \text{Å},\ c \approx 2.55\ \text{Å}\)
- Unit cell contains \(4\) C atoms and \(8\) H atoms.
- Using typical atomic radii for the hard-sphere APF calculation, e.g. \(r_C \approx 0.77\ \text{Å}\) and \(r_H \approx 0.31\ \text{Å}\),

\[
\text{APF}
=
\frac{4\left(\frac{4}{3}\pi r_C^3\right)+8\left(\frac{4}{3}\pi r_H^3\right)}
{abc}
\]

\[
\text{APF}
=
\frac{4\left(\frac{4}{3}\pi(0.77)^3\right)+8\left(\frac{4}{3}\pi(0.31)^3\right)}
{(7.40)(4.95)(2.55)}
\approx 0.093
\]

Using common tabulated atomic-radius conventions can give a very close value, about \(0.088\). Thus, to appropriate precision,

\[
\boxed{0.09}
\]

For a three-point flexural test, the initial slope \(S\) is related to the flexural modulus by

\[
S=\frac{4Eb^3h}{L^3}
\]

so

\[
E=\frac{S L^3}{4 b h^3}
\]

Using \(S=538\times10^3\ \mathrm{N/m}\), \(L=0.060\ \mathrm{m}\), width \(b=0.015\ \mathrm{m}\), and thickness \(h=0.006\ \mathrm{m}\):

\[
E=\frac{(538\times10^3)(0.060)^3}{4(0.015)(0.006)^3}
=8.967\times10^9\ \mathrm{Pa}
=8967\ \mathrm{MPa}
\]

\[
\boxed{8967}
\]

Using the rule of mixtures for mass fractions:

\[
\frac{1}{\rho_c}= \frac{w_{\text{PTFE}}}{\rho_{\text{PTFE}}}+\frac{1-w_{\text{PTFE}}}{\rho_{\text{acetal}}}
\]

Solving for \(w_{\text{PTFE}}\):

\[
w_{\text{PTFE}}
=
\frac{\frac{1}{\rho_{\text{acetal}}}-\frac{1}{\rho_c}}
{\frac{1}{\rho_{\text{acetal}}}-\frac{1}{\rho_{\text{PTFE}}}}
\]

\[
w_{\text{PTFE}}
=
\frac{\frac{1}{1.42}-\frac{1}{1.54}}
{\frac{1}{1.42}-\frac{1}{2.15}}
\approx 0.2295
\]

Thus, the PTFE weight percent is:

\[
\boxed{22.95}
\]

Using the C–C bond energy \(E = 370\ \text{kJ/mol}\), the photon energy per bond is

\[
E_{\text{photon}}=\frac{370\times 10^3}{N_A}\ \text{J}
\]

and

\[
\lambda=\frac{hc}{E_{\text{photon}}}
= \frac{hcN_A}{370\times 10^3}
\approx 3.23\times 10^{-7}\ \text{m}
= 323\ \text{nm}.
\]

\[
\boxed{323}
\]

For steady-state diffusion through the sheet,

\[
J = P\frac{\Delta p}{L}
\]

so

\[
P = J\frac{L}{\Delta p}
\]

Given:

\[
J = 2.2 \times 10^{-8}\ \frac{\text{cm}^3\ \text{STP}}{\text{cm}^2\cdot \text{s}}
\]

\[
L = 50\ \text{mm} = 5\ \text{cm}
\]

\[
\Delta p = 4000 - 2500 = 1500\ \text{kPa} = 1.5 \times 10^6\ \text{Pa}
\]

Thus,

\[
P = \frac{(2.2 \times 10^{-8})(5)}{1.5 \times 10^6}
= 7.33 \times 10^{-14}
\]

\[
\boxed{7.33 \times 10^{-14}}
\]

Using the layer-removal/Stoney relation for equal biaxial curvature,

\[
\sigma=\frac{E t^2}{6(1-\nu)h\rho}
\]

with \(E=4000\ \text{MPa}\), \(t=1.0\ \text{mm}\), \(h=0.025\ \text{mm}\), \(\rho=500\ \text{mm}\), \(\nu=0.4\):

\[
\sigma=\frac{4000(1.0)^2}{6(0.6)(0.025)(500)}
=\frac{4000}{45}\approx 88.9\ \text{MPa}
\]

\[
\boxed{88.9}
\]

For a center-loaded simply supported beam (three-point bending), the effective span is the distance between supports:

\[
L = 5 \text{ in.}
\]

The maximum flexural stress is

\[
\sigma_{\max}=\frac{3PL}{2bh^2}
\]

so the fracture force is

\[
P=\frac{2\sigma_{\max}bh^2}{3L}
\]

\[
P=\frac{2(45000)(0.5)(0.375)^2}{3(5)}
=421.875 \text{ lb}
\]

The elastic central deflection is

\[
\delta=\frac{PL^3}{48EI}, \qquad I=\frac{bh^3}{12}
\]

or equivalently

\[
\delta=\frac{PL^3}{4Ebh^3}
\]

\[
\delta=
\frac{(421.875)(5)^3}{4(18\times10^6)(0.5)(0.375)^3}
=0.02778 \text{ in.}
\]

\[
\boxed{(421.875,\ 0.02778)}
\]

For a rectangular cross-section in three-point bending:

\[
\sigma_f=\frac{3FL}{2bd^2}
=\frac{3(400)(4)}{2(0.50)(0.25)^2}
=7.68\times10^4\ \text{psi}
\]

\[
E_f=\frac{FL^3}{4bd^3\Delta}
=\frac{(400)(4^3)}{4(0.50)(0.25)^3(0.037)}
=2.21\times10^7\ \text{psi}
\]

\[
\boxed{(7.68\times 10^4,\ 2.21\times 10^7)}
\]

The heat-sink performance is based on the material’s volumetric heat capacity, \(\rho c_p\).

For the same heat load and the same temperature rise,

\[
(\rho c_p)_{\text{alumina}} V_{\text{alumina}}
=
(\rho c_p)_{\text{HDPE}} V_{\text{HDPE}}
\]

Using typical room-temperature values:

\[
c_{\text{alumina}} \approx 0.88\ \text{J/g}\cdot^\circ\text{C}
\]
\[
c_{\text{HDPE}} \approx 1.8\text{ to }1.9\ \text{J/g}\cdot^\circ\text{C}
\]
\[
\rho_{\text{HDPE}} \approx 0.95\ \text{g/cm}^3
\]

Since the cross-sectional area is the same,

\[
t_{\text{HDPE}}
=
\frac{\rho_{\text{alumina}}c_{\text{alumina}}}
{\rho_{\text{HDPE}}c_{\text{HDPE}}}
t_{\text{alumina}}
\]

\[
t_{\text{HDPE}}
\approx
\frac{(3.96)(0.88)}{(0.95)(1.85)}(0.02)
\approx 0.04\ \text{cm}
\]

\[
\boxed{0.04}
\]

For stress applied perpendicular to the fiber alignment, use the inverse rule of mixtures:

\[
\frac{1}{E_c}=\frac{V_f}{E_f}+\frac{V_m}{E_m}
\]

\[
E_c=\frac{1}{\frac{0.40}{69}+\frac{0.60}{3.4}}
=5.49 \text{ GPa}
\]

\[
\boxed{5.49}
\]
